{
  "dom_id": "step-q335904",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by \n$s(t)=(4t-12)^{2},$\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in [${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$",
  "worked_solution": "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative.\n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,((4t-12)^{2}) \\\\  & =8(4t-12) \\\\  & =32(t-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ 32(t-3) & <0 \\\\ t-3 & <0 \\\\ t & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$ is\n\n$$\nt\\in [0,\\,3).\n$$",
